id: k8s.scheduler
kind: kubernetes
title: Scheduler
aliases: kube-scheduler, scheduling
tags: architecture, control-plane
questions:
  - why is my pod Pending?
invariant: The scheduler assigns a node to each unbound Pod by filtering feasible nodes (resources, taints, affinity) and scoring the rest; it never starts containers.
answer_30s: The scheduler picks a node (filter by constraints, resources and taints/tolerations, score the survivors) and writes that binding; the kubelet on that node does the actual starting.
answer_2m: |
  Two phases: predicates (does this node fit -- resources, taints and
  tolerations, affinity, node selectors, volume availability) and priorities
  (which good node is best -- spread, bin packing, image locality). Almost
  every Pending Pod is answered by the predicates: insufficient CPU or memory
  against requests, unschedulable taints, a volume that cannot attach (zone or
  ReadWriteOnce conflict), or node selectors that match nothing. The scheduler
  is also why requests matter more than limits: the filter uses requests.
when_to_use:
  - understanding placement, spread and Pending conditions
commands:
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => the FailedScheduling event names the exact predicate that failed
  - kubectl describe node <node> => taints, allocatable capacity, allocated requests
  - kubectl get pods -o wide => which node each Pod actually landed on
failure_modes:
  - requests summing above allocatable, so nothing fits
  - taints without matching tolerations
  - a zonal volume that cannot follow the Pod to another zone
related:
  - k8s.kubelet
  - k8s.resources.requests
  - k8s.pvc
  - k8s.events
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/kube-scheduler/
